---
/**
 * The nominal / constant-dollar switch, with no script behind it.
 *
 * # Why a pair of radios and not a button
 *
 * Neither basis is the default truth. Across FY2020–FY2025 CPI-U rose 25.1%, and statewide cash
 * balances end 9% *above* where they started in nominal dollars and 13% *below* in real ones.
 * Those two sentences support opposite arguments, so the page shows both and names the index. A
 * checkbox would make one of them a modification of the other.
 *
 * Both panels are rendered at build and one is hidden by a sibling selector keyed on which radio
 * is checked. That means the switch works with JavaScript off — which matters more here than the
 * duplicated markup costs, because the whole point of baking the figures into the document was
 * that the document is complete on its own. The previous implementation re-rendered the card
 * from a click handler; a reader without JavaScript got the nominal view and a dead button.
 *
 * `id` must be unique within the page: it names the radio group and both labels point at it.
 *
 * # Why the section address is on the scope and not on the card
 *
 * Both panels are the same card rendered twice, so both carried the same `id` — `#actuals` was on
 * every `/district/*\/finances` page twice, and a browser sent to it scrolls to whichever came
 * first in the document, which is the nominal one whether or not that is the panel the reader has
 * selected. Nothing reported this: the check that reads the built site collected each page's ids
 * into a `Set`, where a duplicate is indistinguishable from the single occurrence it hides behind.
 *
 * So the address belongs one level up. `#actuals` names *the section*, which is one section shown
 * two ways, and the basis is a view of it rather than a different thing to link to. The panels
 * keep their headings and their anchors; the anchors point here.
 */
interface Props {
  /** Names the radio group. Both labels point at it, so it must be unique within the page. */
  id: string;
  /** The section these two panels are, from `SECTIONS` in `src/lib/routes.ts`. */
  section: string;
  /** The year real figures are denominated in, for the label. */
  base: number | null;
}

const { id, section, base } = Astro.props;
---

<div class="basis-scope" id={section} data-part={section}>
  {/*
    The group's name is on each radio, not on a container.

    It was `role="group" aria-label="Dollar basis"` on the `.basis` div below — which holds the two
    labels and no radios, because the inputs have to be siblings of the panels for the sibling
    selectors in `app.css` to switch them with no script. So the name applied to a box containing
    nothing that could be chosen, and the radios themselves announced "Nominal, radio button, 1 of
    2" with no word about what was being chosen between.

    Naming the inputs directly needs no structural change and so keeps the switch working with
    JavaScript off, which is the property this component exists to have. The visible label is a
    suffix of the accessible name, so a reader saying "Nominal" is still naming the control.
  */}
  <input
    class="vh"
    type="radio"
    name={id}
    id={`${id}-nominal`}
    data-basis="nominal"
    aria-label="Dollar basis: Nominal"
    checked
  />
  <input
    class="vh"
    type="radio"
    name={id}
    id={`${id}-real`}
    data-basis="real"
    aria-label="Dollar basis: Constant dollars"
  />
  <div class="basis">
    <label for={`${id}-nominal`} data-basis="nominal">Nominal</label>
    <label for={`${id}-real`} data-basis="real">Constant dollars</label>
    <span class="n">{base == null ? "no index covers this series" : `FY${base} dollars`}</span>
  </div>
  <div class="basis-panel nominal"><slot name="nominal" /></div>
  <div class="basis-panel real"><slot name="real" /></div>
</div>
